<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1918 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>South Africa's anthem in the years of apartheid. Since 1997 a part of it is sung within the country's new, shared anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>C. J. Langenhoven</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Marthinus Lourens de Villiers</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Afrikaners; the Union and then the Republic of South Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Afrikaans, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>South Africa</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem May 1918; music 1921; co-national anthem from 1938, sole anthem 1957–1994</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by C. J. Langenhoven (1918; he died in 1932), music by M. L. de Villiers (1921))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In May 1918 C. J. Langenhoven wrote an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> poem called &quot;Die Stem&quot; (&quot;The Voice&quot;). The Reverend Marthinus Lourens de Villiers set it to music in 1921; the tune that was accepted was his second, since the first did not satisfy Langenhoven. In the 1920s the South African Broadcasting Corporation played it at the close of each day's broadcasts, together with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;. Betty Steyn first recorded it, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1926, and it was first sung in public on 31 May 1928, at a raising of the new South African flag. The poem first had three verses; at the government's request Langenhoven added a fourth, on a religious theme.</p><p>In 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> proclaimed it one of two co-national anthems, alongside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;. From 1952 it was sung in English as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, both versions having official status. &quot;God Save the Queen&quot; was dropped as a co-national anthem in May 1957, leaving &quot;Die Stem&quot; the sole national anthem from 1957 to 1994; the British anthem remained the royal anthem until 1961, when South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> ceased to be a Commonwealth realm.</p><p>These were the years of apartheid, and the song became bound up with it. Black South Africans generally disliked it, seeing it as triumphalist, and strongly associated it with the apartheid regime. P. W. Botha, state president in the 1980s, was fond of it and had his entourage sing it on a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>As apartheid was dismantled in the early 1990s, its use dwindled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> (ANC) regarded it as offensive because of its link with apartheid and minority rule, and it was agreed that it would not be sung at rugby matches; yet at a test match against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1992 the crowd sang it during a silence for victims of political violence, and a recording was played over the loudspeakers, which caused controversy. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics">1992 Summer Olympics</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> the South African team used &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a>&quot; and a neutral flag instead. In 1993 a commission considered 119 entries for a new anthem and chose none. After the 1994 general election &quot;Die Stem&quot; kept its official status, sharing it equally with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a>&quot;, long a hymn of the ANC. When the old flag was lowered for the last time at Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, &quot;Die Stem&quot; was sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> and English as the new flag was raised, and a black choir sang it at the 1994 presidential inauguration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and at the 1995 Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> final.</p><p>Playing two anthems could take five minutes. In 1997, with the new constitution, a single national anthem was adopted that joins part of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a>&quot; and part of &quot;Die Stem&quot;; it is still in use. The song's place in it remains debated: some South Africans want its lines removed because of their link with apartheid, while others defend them as part of the reconciliation that followed. In 2012, when organisers in the United Kingdom mistakenly played &quot;Die Stem&quot; at a women's field hockey match, the South African team and staff reacted with outrage. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> version remains popular with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalists and far-right groups such as the Afrikaner Weerstandsbeweging, and in the apartheid era &quot;Die Stem&quot; was also the name of a far-right periodical.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An uplifting hymn of devotion to the fatherland and to God. One verse pledges dedication to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> (left out of the English version) and another recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>; the fourth verse, added at the government's request, is religious.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Afrikaans</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Afrikaans</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Uit die blou van onse hemel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Out of the blue of our heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Uit die diepte van ons see,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>out of the depth of our sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Oor ons ewige gebergtes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over our everlasting mountain ranges</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Waar die kranse antwoord gee.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where the cliffs give answer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Deur ons vêr-verlate vlaktes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through our far-forsaken plains</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Met die kreun van ossewa –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with the groaning of the ox-wagon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ruis die stem van ons geliefde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>murmurs the voice of our beloved,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Van ons land Suid-Afrika.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of our land South Africa.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ons sal antwoord op jou roepstem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall answer your call,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ons sal offer wat jy vra:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall offer what you ask:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ons sal lewe, ons sal sterwe –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall live, we shall die,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ons vir jou, Suid-Afrika.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we for you, South Africa.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In die murg van ons gebeente,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the marrow of our bones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In ons hart en siel en gees,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in our heart and soul and spirit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In ons roem op ons verlede,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in our pride in our past,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In ons hoop op wat sal wees.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in our hope for what will be,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In ons wil en werk en wandel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in our will and work and way of life,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Van ons wieg tot aan ons graf –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from our cradle to our grave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Deel geen ander land ons liefde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no other land shares our love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Trek geen ander trou ons af.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no other loyalty draws us away.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Vaderland! Ons sal die adel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fatherland! We shall bear the nobility</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Van jou naam met ere dra:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of your name with honour:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Waar en trou as Afrikaners –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>true and faithful as Afrikaners,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Kinders van Suid-Afrika.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>children of South Africa.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 48 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In die songloed van ons somer,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the sun-glow of our summer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In ons winternag se kou,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the cold of our winter night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In die lente van ons liefde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the spring of our love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">In die lanfer van ons rou.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the crape of our mourning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">By die klink van huw'liks-klokkies,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the ringing of wedding bells,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">By die kluit-klap op die kis –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at the thud of the clod on the coffin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Streel jou stem ons nooit verniet nie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your voice never caresses us in vain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Weet jy waar jou kinders is.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you know where your children are.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Op jou roep sê ons nooit nee nie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To your call we never say no,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Sê ons altyd, altyd ja:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we always, always say yes:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Om te lewe, om te sterwe –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to live, to die,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Ja, ons kom, Suid-Afrika.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>yes, we come, South Africa.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Op U Almag vas vertrouend</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Firmly trusting in Your almighty power</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Het ons vadere gebou:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our fathers built:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Skenk ook ons die krag, o Here!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give us too the strength, O Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Om te handhaaf en te hou –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to uphold and to keep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Dat die erwe van ons vad're</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that the heritage of our fathers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Vir ons kinders erwe bly:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>remain a heritage for our children:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Knegte van die Allerhoogste,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>servants of the Most High,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Teen die hele wêreld vry.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>free against the whole world.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Soos ons vadere vertrou het,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As our fathers trusted,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Leer ook ons vertrou, o Heer –</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>teach us too to trust, O Lord:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Met ons land en met ons nasie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with our land and with our nation</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="af">Sal dit wel wees, God regeer.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it will be well, God reigns.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: C. J. Langenhoven (1918); the Afrikaans text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Stem_van_Suid-Afrika" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.7: 'ruis', to rustle, murmur or sound like the sea or wind.</li><li>line 2.5: 'wandel', one's walk, conduct, way of life.</li><li>line 2.8: 'Trek geen ander trou ons af', literally 'no other loyalty pulls us away'.</li><li>line 3.4: 'lanfer', black crape worn in mourning.</li><li>line 3.6: 'kluit-klap op die kis', the thud of clods of earth thrown on the coffin.</li><li>lines 4.5-4.6: 'erwe', inheritance, heritage (also plots of land).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
